# SINGLE-OWNER SUB-PLACE POS — THE ACCEPT CONTROL. The remedy the widened
# diagnostic prescribes must COMPILE and RUN, on both spellings the suite
# rejects: a struct field and a tuple element (both spellings of the element).
# A reject-only suite cannot tell "the gate works" from "the gate rejects
# everything", and a remedy nobody verified is a diagnostic that sends users
# into a wall.
#
# `.clone()` is the ONLY in-language remedy at a sub-place — `^h.f` is
# `E_PartialMove` under D10(a) ADDENDUM (2026-09-02); see the sibling cell.
#
# Each `.clone()` materialises its own closure env, so there is one owner per
# binding and NO double-free — which is the property this cell exists to pin.
#
# ⚠ It is NOT `--sanitize`-clean, and that is a KNOWN, FILED, SEQUENCED gap, not
# a defect of the remedy: LeakSanitizer reports 16 B from `__gorget_closure_env_alloc`
# because a struct holding a `Callable` field is `[drop: None]` and never drops
# it — todo/t0948, whose own hard gate names THIS reject as its prerequisite
# ("does not land without a read-side materializer … landing WITH it"). So the
# trade this track makes is not "working program -> leaking program"; it is
# "a construct that is memory-unsafe in general -> a compile error plus a 16 B
# leak already filed", which is strictly better on the severity ranking. Wire
# this cell on stdout, and DO NOT add it to a sanitizer battery until t0948
# lands.

struct Holder:
    Callable[int()] f

Callable[int()] mk(int n):
    return (): n + 1

void main():
    Holder h = Holder(mk(40))
    Callable[int()] g = h.f.clone()
    print(g())
    (int, Callable[int()]) t = (1, mk(50))
    Callable[int()] gt = t.1.clone()
    print(gt())
    Callable[int()] gu = t._1.clone()
    print(gu())
